.SH NAME
ktm-guide-memory \- Memory (chapter 9 of the ktm language guide)
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
Where values live, how to allocate memory that outlives a function, how to give it back, and the habits that keep a program free of leaks. ktm has no garbage collector: memory you allocate is yours until you free it.
.SH Three places a value lives
.IP \(bu 2
\fBLocals\fR live on the stack, from their declaration to the end of their block. They cost nothing to create and are freed automatically.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBGlobals\fR and \fBstatic\fR locals live for the whole program.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBHeap memory\fR lives from the moment you allocate it until you free it. Use it when a value must outlive the function that makes it, or when its size is known only at run time.
.SH Allocating and freeing
.PP
The mem (see ktm-mem(3)) module allocates, resizes and frees.
.PP
.TS
